diagnosis_text:
Affected component: r12’s connected interfaces advertising user subnets into EIGRP. Mechanism: r12 originates 102.1.x.0/24 networks into EIGRP, and r11 redistributes EIGRP into OSPF toward r1. If a connected prefix on r12 goes down, EIGRP withdraws it; r11 then withdraws the corresponding OSPF external LSA, and r1 loses the route from its RIB. Localization: On r12, only 102.1.1.0/24 remains connected on the loopback (lo) and is advertised. The other three subnets 102.1.2.0/24, 102.1.3.0/24, and 102.1.4.0/24 are configured on lo1, lo2, and lo3, which are DOWN. Evidence: r12 IP state shows lo1/lo2/lo3 DOWN with 102.1.2.1/24, 102.1.3.1/24, 102.1.4.1/24 (ip addr); these routes are absent from r12’s routing table; EIGRP topology on r12 reports “% Network not in table” for 102.1.2.0/24. Meanwhile, the EIGRP adjacency to r11 remains up, matching the symptom. Root cause: The loopback interfaces lo1, lo2, and lo3 on r12 are administratively down, removing the connected routes and causing their selective withdrawal from EIGRP and, via redistribution on r11, from OSPF on r1. Corrective fix: Bring up the loopback interfaces or place the addresses on an always-up interface. For example on r12: ip link set lo1 up; ip link set lo2 up; ip link set lo3 up (or move 102.1.2/24–102.1.4/24 onto lo). Ensure EIGRP continues to advertise them (network statements already present), after which r11 will redistribute them back into OSPF and r1’s RIB will restore the three prefixes. === END ===